# Confirms wc_dismiss_for_natural_fire(): if the conflict window's own
# timer reaches its REAL end time while the window is still open and
# undecided (only possible because the app stayed in the foreground the
# whole time), the conflict window should get out of the way and the real
# alarm/ring screen should take over cleanly - not stack on top of it, and
# not leave the conflict window stuck on screen past the real fire time.
#
# Uses a short remaining time (well under a minute) on purpose: too short
# for wakeup_find_early_slot() to find any free minute before it, so
# try_auto_early_wake() also can't resolve it silently, and the
# single-row "Ok" informational window appears instead, with the red
# "Timer conflicts with / another app's wakeup. / MUST STAY OPEN." message.
#
# This isn't alignment-dependent luck - wakeup_find_early_slot()'s first
# search candidate is `(end_time/60)*60 - 60` (deliberately a full extra
# minute before the minute-boundary nearest end_time, not just the
# nearest one), and the search immediately gives up once that candidate
# is <= the real "now". Working through the arithmetic for a duration D
# (< 60s) against every possible wall-clock alignment, the worst-case
# margin between that first candidate and "now" is exactly `60 - D`
# seconds - always positive for any D < 60, so the search is
# *guaranteed* to find zero backward candidates regardless of timing,
# not just "usually" - no TestBlockedSystemWakeupMinuteOffsetsNeg
# simulation needed to force it. (At D=15 here, that's a comfortable
# 45s guaranteed margin.)
#
# NOTE: live-verified (2026-10-01) - both TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay
# values below are measured from live runs, not guessed. The Stop button
# on the alarm screen is DOWN (confirmed).
#
# IMPORTANT: a TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay override is PER-TIMER and
# PERSISTS until explicitly resent for that same index - there is no
# "clear" message. This file's checkpoint 2 used to send no override at
# all, on the theory that the alarm screen's overtime counter would then
# reflect real, live elapsed time (matching alarm_overtime_display.seq's
# convention) - but checkpoint 1 already set an override for this same
# timer (idx 0), and because it's never cleared, checkpoint 2 was
# silently still governed by checkpoint 1's STALE value the whole time.
# Concretely: format_alarm_elapsed() computes `effective_now_for(idx,t) -
# end_time`, and with checkpoint 1's override still active that's always
# `-11`, clamped to 0 by the elapsed formula - so checkpoint 2 displayed a
# constant "+0:00" no matter how long the SLEEP before it was (live-
# verified repeatedly while tracking this down, including at 10s, 16s,
# and 19s waits - all three produced identical "+0:00" until this was
# understood). A diagnostic run with checkpoint 1's override removed
# confirmed the real, live value at checkpoint 2 is "+0:14" (close to the
# expected ~15s given this file's 15s duration and normal CLI round-trip
# timing) - checkpoint 2 below now sends its own fresh override (negative
# = overtime, per effective_now_for()'s sign convention) instead of
# relying on "no override = live", since that assumption doesn't hold
# once an earlier checkpoint in the same file has touched the same index.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq
APPMSG --string 10027="0"

APPMSG --string 10000="$(printf 'Quick Timer\03715\0371')"
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10021=1 10022=15
SLEEP 1
# Value measured from a live run (backdoor-set 15 at T0, minus real
# elapsed seconds observed) - chosen specifically to keep the tolerance
# a smaller fraction of the value than this file's earlier 8s duration
# did, without needing the minute-long waits the much-longer-duration
# files elsewhere in this suite use (see the derivation above for why
# 15s is still safely under the 60s threshold that would require extra
# blocked-minute simulation).
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10029=11 10030=5 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT conflict_menu_ok_no_early_slot

# Let real wall-clock time cross the timer's actual 15s end while the
# conflict window (still undecided) sits on screen and the app stays
# foregrounded the entire time, then wc_dismiss_for_natural_fire() should
# swap in the real alarm screen. Re-override the same index with a fresh,
# measured overtime value (see the header comment above for why this is
# required rather than just omitting the override) - generous tolerance
# since this value depends on real CLI round-trip timing, not a backdoor.
#
# The -14±5 here was right at the edge of its own tolerance window: a
# solo containerized run measured +0:10 real overtime (barely inside the
# old 9-19s window), so ordinary run-to-run jitter could easily push it
# to BAD, as observed 2026-10-01 in a full-suite run. Recentered on the
# measured value with more margin.
SLEEP 19
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10029=-10 10030=7 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT should_now_show_real_alarm_not_conflict_menu

TAP down
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT final_state
